# Young adults (part b): facilitator scripts, tasks and witness stories.
R = {
'YA-3-1': dict(
  saint="Brother Lawrence: a wounded soldier turned monastery cook who learned to pray among the pots and pans.",
  tryit=["Try three minutes of silent prayer.", "Note what happens.", "Write one realistic practice for this week."],
  saintStory="Nicolas Herman was born in 1614 in Lorraine, France. As a young man he joined the army, and in the Thirty Years’ War he was badly wounded and left with a permanent limp. He worked as a footman. At eighteen, he had an experience while looking at a bare tree in winter, thinking of how it would leaf in spring: a deep awareness of God’s providence, which never left him.\nIn 1640 he entered the Discalced Carmelite monastery in Paris as a lay brother, taking the name Lawrence of the Resurrection. He was assigned to the kitchen, which he hated at first, then to repair sandals. He spent over forty years in ordinary work. He found that he could be in the presence of God while peeling vegetables, as much as at the chapel. He wrote: “The time of business does not with me differ from the time of prayer; and in the noise and clatter of my kitchen… I possess God in as great tranquility as if I were upon my knees at the blessed sacrament.”\nHe didn’t write a book; his conversations and letters were collected after he died in 1691 as The Practice of the Presence of God. Many people over three centuries have found them helpful.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Be honest: how is your prayer life right now? One word.” Go around the group (answers like ‘dry,’ ‘nonexistent,’ ‘rushed,’ ‘good’). Say, “We’ll talk about why prayer is hard, and what helps.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism defines prayer as ‘the raising of one’s mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God.’ (CCC 2559) It’s a relationship with a Person who loves us, who comes to meet us: ‘Prayer is the encounter of God’s thirst with ours.’ (CCC 2560, quoting Augustine) Even the desire to pray is a gift. So the first step is to receive: God is already seeking you. Prayer isn’t performance or technique, though it has methods. It involves vocal prayer, meditation and contemplation. It involves petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise and adoration. And it includes silence and listening.”", "Say: “Why is it hard? CCC 2726-2728 and common experience: Distractions: our minds wander. Dryness: we feel nothing. Doubt: ‘Is anyone there?’ Busyness: no time. Boredom or acedia: spiritual laziness. Discouragement: ‘I’m not good at this.’ Pride or perfectionism: wanting results. Wrong expectations: wanting consolation instead of God. Brother Lawrence had these too; he said that for the first ten years he suffered much. The Church’s guidance: persevere. Prayer is a battle (CCC 2725), and a gift. Fidelity matters more than feeling. Start small: five minutes a day is better than an hour once a month. Choose a time and place. Use a simple structure: begin with the Sign of the Cross, a prayer to the Spirit, read a short Scripture passage, speak in your own words, be silent, end with the Our Father.”", "Say: “The practice of the presence of God: offer ordinary tasks to God, remember him briefly through the day (a short phrase like ‘Jesus, I trust in you’), and turn back gently when you forget. Thérèse called prayer ‘a surge of the heart, a simple look turned toward heaven.’ Jesus’ own teaching: go to your room and shut the door (Matthew 6:6), don’t babble (6:7), pray the Our Father, ask persistently (Luke 11:5-13), and pray always (Luke 18:1). Pray with others and alone. Use the Church’s prayers. Not feeling anything doesn’t mean prayer isn’t working. Many saints have gone through long dryness. Look at fruits over time: more patience, love, honesty. If you struggle, talk to a priest or a spiritual director.”"],
    ask=["How does the Catechism define prayer? (Raising the mind and heart to God; the encounter of God’s thirst with ours.)", "Name two obstacles to prayer. (Distraction, dryness, busyness, doubt.)", "What is the practice of the presence of God? (Remembering God throughout the day and offering ordinary tasks to him.)"],
    practice=["Try three minutes of silent prayer together after reading Psalm 23 slowly. Then each person writes one sentence: what happened?", "In pairs, share your biggest obstacle and one thing that has helped.", "Write a simple rule: when, where and how long you will pray this week.", "Choose a short phrase to repeat during the day, such as ‘Jesus, I trust in you.’"],
    close="Close with a minute of silence, then the Our Father slowly. Sign of the Cross. Invite each person to text or tell a partner at the end of the week how it went.")),

'YA-3-2': dict(
  saint="Guigo II the Carthusian: a monk who set down the four steps of sacred reading.",
  tryit=["Practice the four steps on one passage.", "Write what you noticed at each step.", "Choose a passage for the week."],
  saintStory="Guigo II was a Carthusian monk who became prior of the Grande Chartreuse, in the French Alps, around 1174, and died around 1188. The Carthusians lived in solitude, silence and prayer, reading and copying books. He wrote a short letter to a fellow monk named Gervase, known as the Scala Claustralium, “The Ladder of Monks.”\nHe described the spiritual life as a four-rung ladder: reading (lectio), meditation (meditatio), prayer (oratio) and contemplation (contemplatio). He compared them to eating. Reading is like putting food in the mouth, meditation chews it, prayer tastes it, and contemplation is the sweetness that nourishes. “Reading without meditation is arid; meditation without reading is liable to error; prayer without meditation is lukewarm; meditation without prayer is unfruitful; prayer with devotion leads to contemplation, but to obtain contemplation without prayer would be rare.”\nHis letter turned a long tradition of monastic reading into a clear method that has been used ever since, and which Pope Benedict XVI recommended to the whole Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand out a short passage (Luke 5:1-11 or Psalm 131). Say: “Read this as you would read an email.” Allow one minute. Ask, “What did you retain?” Then say, “Now we will read it as a letter from someone who loves us.”",
    teach=["Say: “Lectio divina, ‘divine reading,’ is an ancient way of praying with Scripture. Dei Verbum 25: ‘Prayer should accompany the reading of Sacred Scripture, so that God and man may talk together.’ Benedict XVI, in 2005, said that if lectio divina is promoted effectively, ‘it will bring to the Church—I am convinced of it—a new spiritual springtime.’ The method follows Guigo. Lectio: read slowly, preferably aloud, twice. Notice words, images and actions. Meditatio: ask what it says; ponder a word or phrase; connect it to your life. Mary ‘kept all these things, pondering them in her heart.’ Oratio: respond to God in your own words—praise, thanks, sorrow, petition. Contemplatio: rest quietly in God’s presence, beyond words. Some add actio: resolve to act on what you received.”", "Say: “Why this way? Scripture isn’t just information; it’s God speaking. ‘The word of God is living and active.’ (Hebrews 4:12) Lectio treats the text as a person to meet. It engages the mind and the heart. It slows us down in a fast culture. It also protects us: reading in the Church’s context, with attention to the Catechism and the Church’s teaching, avoids private distortions. The Church’s liturgy is the other great school: the Mass readings. Pairing both: read the Sunday Gospel in advance, using lectio. Also: Ambrose and Augustine; Augustine’s own conversion started when he heard ‘Take and read.’ A good choice of passages: Gospel scenes, Psalms, the letters of Paul in small pieces. Avoid reading randomly or hunting for guidance in verses. Start with a Gospel: Mark or Luke.”", "Say: “Practical tips. Choose a regular time, 10 to 20 minutes. Find a quiet place. Begin with a prayer to the Holy Spirit. Use a good Catholic Bible with notes. Keep a journal. Don’t aim for insights every time; fidelity matters. Distractions: gently return to the text. Dryness: stay with the words. If a phrase strikes you, stay on it. Share with others: lectio can be done in groups (the Benedictine form): read the passage three times, each time share a word, an image and an invitation. In the Ignatian form, you imagine yourself in the scene. Over time, the habit of lectio shapes your thinking. Mary treasured the Word. Let it become the way you pray.”"],
    ask=["What are the four steps of lectio divina? (Read, meditate, pray, contemplate.)", "What does Dei Verbum 25 say about prayer and Scripture? (Prayer should accompany reading so that God and man may talk together.)", "How does reading in the Church protect us? (It guards against private distortion.)"],
    practice=["Choose Luke 5:1-11. Read it aloud slowly twice. Let each person name a word or phrase that stands out.", "Silent meditation, five minutes: write what the passage says to you.", "Write a short prayer in response, then rest in silence for two minutes.", "Share one insight aloud in pairs and choose a passage for daily lectio this week."],
    close="Pray the Our Father slowly. Sign of the Cross. Invite everyone to practice lectio with the coming Sunday’s Gospel.")),

'YA-3-3': dict(
  saint="Carlo Acutis: a teenager from Milan who called the Eucharist his highway to heaven.",
  tryit=["Walk through the Mass in four movements.", "Say what you offer at each.", "Plan to prepare for next Sunday."],
  saintStory="Carlo Acutis was born in London in 1991 and grew up in Milan. He was an ordinary teenager who loved computers, soccer, video games and his dog. His family was not particularly devout, but he asked at seven to receive First Communion. From then on, he went to Mass every day, and prayed the Rosary and made Eucharistic adoration. He said, “The Eucharist is my highway to heaven.”\nHe taught himself computer coding and created a website cataloguing Eucharistic miracles around the world, which has since been exhibited in many countries. He helped the homeless, was kind to classmates who were left out, and used his Sunday allowance for others.\nIn October 2006, at fifteen, he was diagnosed with acute leukemia. He offered his suffering for the pope and the Church. He died on October 12, 2006. He was beatified in 2020 and canonized in 2025. He is called the first millennial saint, and he shows that the Mass can be the center of a young person’s life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Invite the group to arrive five minutes early to a Mass this week as an experiment, or show a short video clip of a Mass beginning. Ask them to notice what they feel in the first five minutes. Share the impressions.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism: ‘The Eucharist is ‘the source and summit of the Christian life.’’ (CCC 1324) In the Mass, Christ’s sacrifice on Calvary is made present, and we are united to it. The Mass has two main parts: the Liturgy of the Word and the Liturgy of the Eucharist, framed by Introductory and Concluding Rites. We can think of four movements: Gathering (we come as a community, confess our sins, sing, pray the Collect); Listening (readings, psalm, Gospel, homily, creed, prayers of the faithful); Offering and Consecration (gifts brought, the Eucharistic Prayer: thanks, memorial, Spirit, consecration, intercessions, doxology); Communion and Sending (Our Father, sign of peace, Lamb of God, reception of Christ, thanksgiving, blessing and dismissal).”", "Say: “Participation: Vatican II urged ‘full, conscious and active participation.’ (SC 14) This doesn’t mean a lot of activity but an interior engagement: listening, singing, responding, offering. At the offertory, offer yourself: your week, your work, your struggles, with the bread and wine. At the consecration, adore. At Communion, receive. Then give thanks. A helpful rhythm: before Mass (arrive a few minutes early, read the Sunday readings, ask for a particular grace), during Mass (be present), after (silent thanksgiving, then live it out). Common obstacles: distraction, routine, unfamiliarity, poor music or preaching. Tips: sit near the front; follow along with the missal; pray the responses; bring an intention; receive reverently.”", "Say: “Why bother on Sunday? The Church teaches that Catholics are obliged to attend Mass on Sundays and holy days (CCC 2180-2183) because it is the center of our relationship with God, and because we are a body. This is not an arbitrary rule but a response to love. Deliberately missing is grave; illness or serious obstacle excuses. In the Eucharist, we receive Christ and are united with the Church. The Eucharist transforms us and sends us. ‘Ite, missa est.’ Carlo’s secret was very simple: he went to Mass, adored, and let it shape him. He said, ‘All people are born as originals but many die as photocopies.’ Don’t let routine kill the wonder. Try daily Mass or Eucharistic adoration sometime. Receive worthily: in a state of grace, after a one-hour fast. If you’ve been away from the sacraments, come back through Confession.”"],
    ask=["What are the four movements of the Mass? (Gathering, listening, offering and consecration, communion and sending.)", "What does full, conscious, active participation mean? (An interior engagement: listening, responding, offering.)", "Why is Sunday Mass obligatory? (It is the center of our relationship with God and the Church.)"],
    practice=["Use a missal or Mass outline. Walk through the four movements, and write at each what you will offer.", "In pairs, share what helps you pay attention, and what distracts you.", "Plan your preparation for next Sunday: read the readings in advance, arrive early, bring an intention.", "Write a short thanksgiving prayer for after Communion."],
    close="Pray the Anima Christi together. Sign of the Cross. Invite the group to attend one extra Mass or a holy hour this month.")),

'YA-4-1': dict(
  saint="Augustine at sixteen: the pear tree that taught him what sin is for.",
  tryit=["Write about a time you did something wrong for no good reason.", "Name what you were really after.", "Say where you needed mercy."],
  saintStory="In Book Two of his Confessions, Augustine recalls a night when he was sixteen. He and a gang of friends went to steal pears from a neighbor’s tree. They weren’t hungry; they had better pears at home. They took a few bites, and threw most of them to the pigs. “It was foul, and I loved it. I loved my own undoing. I loved my error—not that for which I erred, but the error itself.”\nHe analyzed it for pages. Why would he do something so pointless? He realized that on his own, he wouldn’t have done it; it was the company, the thrill of belonging, the desire to do wrong simply because it was forbidden. It showed him that sin is not just breaking a rule but loving something disordered, a twisted imitation of freedom.\nAugustine wrote his Confessions as a prayer to God, years later, after his conversion. He used the story not to excuse himself but to confess it. It is an honest look at how sin works. He wrote, “Thou hast made us for thyself, and our heart is restless until it rests in thee.” The Confessions are the first Western autobiography.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “What is the difference between making a mistake, hurting someone by accident, and sinning?” Take answers. Then, “Why do you think a sixteen-year-old would steal fruit he didn’t even want?” Let the group suggest reasons.",
    teach=["Say: “Sin is ‘an offense against reason, truth and right conscience; it is a failure in genuine love for God and neighbor’ (CCC 1849). It’s first of all about a relationship: ‘Against you, you only, have I sinned.’ (Psalm 51:4) Sin harms our relationship with God, with others and ourselves. It isn’t just breaking rules, but misdirected love. The Church distinguishes original sin (the wounded condition we inherit from the first sin; Baptism removes its guilt but leaves concupiscence, the inclination to sin), and personal sin. Personal sins can be mortal or venial. Mortal sin has three conditions: grave matter, full knowledge, and deliberate consent. It destroys charity in the heart and separates us from God’s grace. Venial sin wounds charity but doesn’t destroy it.”", "Say: “What does sin do? It enslaves: ‘Everyone who commits sin is a slave to sin.’ (John 8:34) Repeated sins form habits, called vices, and the capital sins: pride, greed, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony and sloth. Sin damages others and creates ‘structures of sin’ in society. It also leaves shame and hiding: Adam and Eve hid after eating the fruit. It makes us blind to our own faults. The deadliest thing is when we stop seeing sin at all. John Paul II said the loss of the sense of sin is the sin of our age. Our culture tends to explain away sin as psychology, society, genetics, or says that ‘there are no moral rules.’ It’s true that many factors affect our freedom (upbringing, addiction, trauma), and the Church recognizes that these can reduce culpability. But it doesn’t eliminate responsibility; to deny sin also denies freedom and dignity.”", "Say: “Why talk about sin, then? Because the Gospel begins with it: ‘Repent, and believe in the gospel.’ (Mark 1:15) Naming the problem is the first step to the cure. Sin is not the last word: ‘Where sin increased, grace abounded all the more.’ (Romans 5:20) The Church says: God’s mercy is greater than all sin; no sin is beyond forgiveness except refusing forgiveness. Honesty about sin is not self-hatred but truth. We ask: What am I really seeking in this? Augustine found the answer: ‘Thou hast made us for thyself.’ Every sin is a misdirected search for a good that only God satisfies. The antidote is honesty, repentance, and returning to the Father (Luke 15). A healthy examination of conscience is not scrupulosity; it’s a habit of looking at our life in God’s light.”"],
    ask=["How does the Catechism define sin? (An offense against reason, truth and right conscience; a failure of love for God and neighbor.)", "What are the three conditions for a mortal sin? (Grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent.)", "What is Augustine’s point in the pear story? (Sin can be loved for itself; it’s a twisted search for freedom.)"],
    practice=["Write briefly about a time you did something you knew was wrong, and ask: What was I really seeking? Share only what you choose to.", "In pairs, discuss why we hide sin and what helps us tell the truth.", "Read Psalm 51:1-12 slowly and underline the lines that speak to you.", "Write a one-line prayer of honesty to God."],
    close="Pray Psalm 51:10-12 together. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to do a short examination of conscience nightly this week.")),

'YA-4-2': dict(
  saint="John Nepomuk: a Bohemian priest who, according to tradition, died rather than break the seal of confession.",
  tryit=["Walk through the rite of Confession.", "Write an honest examination of conscience.", "Plan when you will go."],
  saintStory="John of Nepomuk was born around 1345 in Bohemia. He studied law, became a priest and served as vicar general of the archdiocese of Prague. He was a skilled administrator and a popular preacher. King Wenceslaus IV was a violent, unstable ruler who quarreled with the archbishop over church property and appointments.\nA later tradition said that John was the confessor of the queen, and that the jealous king demanded that he reveal what she had told him in confession. John refused. He was arrested, tortured, and thrown from the Charles Bridge into the Vltava River on March 20, 1393. Historians think the real cause was the quarrel over a church appointment, but the tradition of the seal grew strong and he was canonized in 1729.\nWhatever the historical details, John became the patron saint of confessors and of bridges. His statue stands on many bridges in Europe. Priests have indeed died rather than reveal confessions, and the Church’s law is absolute: the seal can never be broken.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Be honest: when did you last go to Confession? What do you remember about it? What gets in the way?” Give the group a moment of silence, then invite a few answers, without judgment. Some may say they have never been or feel afraid.",
    teach=["Say: “Reconciliation is the sacrament in which Christ forgives the sins committed after Baptism through the ministry of the Church. Its roots: John 20:21-23, where the risen Jesus breathes on the apostles and says, ‘If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven.’ Also Matthew 18:18 and 2 Corinthians 5:18-20. The early Church practiced it, first in public and later privately. The Council of Trent (1551) taught that Christ instituted the sacrament. Why confess to a priest? Because Christ gave this power to the apostles and their successors; because sin harms the Church too; because naming sin aloud breaks its power; and because we need to hear forgiveness spoken. The priest acts in persona Christi.”", "Say: “The rite. The penitent prepares by an examination of conscience (use the Ten Commandments, the Beatitudes, or a guide). In the confessional: Sign of the Cross; ‘Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. It has been ___ since my last confession.’ Confess your sins clearly, simply, with number and kind of mortal sins; the priest may offer counsel; he gives a penance (a prayer or act); you say an Act of Contrition; the priest gives absolution: ‘I absolve you from your sins in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.’ Then thanksgiving and do your penance. Required elements: contrition (sorrow, with the intention to amend), confession, satisfaction. Imperfect contrition (fear of punishment) is enough for the sacrament. The seal of confession is absolute: the priest can never reveal what he hears, and the penalty for breaking it is automatic excommunication. Face-to-face or behind a screen are both options.”", "Say: “Common fears. ‘I’ve forgotten how.’ Tell the priest; he’ll guide you. ‘My sins are too bad.’ No sin is beyond God’s mercy. ‘The priest will judge me.’ Priests hear confessions regularly and are there to help. ‘I’ll just confess to God.’ You can and should pray, but Christ gave us this sacrament for certainty of forgiveness. ‘I keep confessing the same sins.’ That’s common; it’s part of the process, and a sign you’re fighting. Frequency: the Church requires confession of mortal sins at least once a year, and before Communion if in mortal sin; frequent confession (monthly) is recommended even for venial sins for growth. Effects: reconciliation with God and the Church, peace of conscience, spiritual strength, restoring grace. The Church also offers sacramental seal for everyone, and John Nepomuk’s story signals how serious it is. Pope Francis: ‘Confession is not a torture chamber but the place of mercy.’”"],
    ask=["Who has the power to forgive sins in the sacrament? (Christ, through the priest.)", "What are the acts of the penitent? (Contrition, confession, satisfaction.)", "What is the seal of confession? (The priest may never reveal anything heard in confession.)"],
    practice=["Use a guide to write a private examination of conscience.", "In pairs, walk through the rite aloud, taking turns as penitent and priest, with a generic script.", "Memorize or read the Act of Contrition.", "Decide when you will go, and put it on your calendar."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition together. Sign of the Cross. Offer to go together to Confession, or share the parish confession times.")),

'YA-4-3': dict(
  saint="Corrie ten Boom: a Dutch watchmaker’s daughter who hid Jews, survived Ravensbrück, and later forgave a guard. (A Reformed Christian, honored by Christians of all traditions.)",
  tryit=["Write the name of someone you struggle to forgive.", "Write what forgiveness is and what it is not.", "Choose one act of mercy toward them or another."],
  saintStory="Corrie ten Boom was born in 1892 in Haarlem, Netherlands, the daughter of a watchmaker. Her family was devout Dutch Reformed, and they believed it was their duty to help Jews. After the Nazi occupation, they converted part of their house into a secret hiding place. They sheltered Jews and Dutch resistance members. In February 1944, someone betrayed them and the Gestapo arrested the family. Her father died soon after. Corrie and her sister Betsie were sent to Ravensbrück concentration camp. Betsie died there in December 1944. Corrie was released a week later, due to a clerical error.\nAfter the war, she traveled the world, preaching forgiveness. In 1947, after speaking in a church in Munich, a man came to her and held out his hand. She recognized him as one of the guards from Ravensbrück, a cruel man. He said he had become a Christian, and asked for her forgiveness. She froze. “I stood there with the coldness clutching my heart. But forgiveness is not an emotion. I knew that. Forgiveness is an act of the will… ‘Jesus, help me!’ I prayed silently. ‘I can lift my hand. I can do that much. You supply the feeling.’” She took his hand. “I forgive you, brother!” she cried.\nShe died in 1983 at ninety-one.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Forgiveness is easy to talk about and hard to do. Think of one person who hurt you. You don’t have to say their name. How does it feel to think of them?” Allow silence for a minute, then, “What do you think forgiveness means? What doesn’t it mean?” Write answers.",
    teach=["Say: “We receive mercy in order to give it. Jesus: ‘Be merciful, even as your Father is merciful.’ (Luke 6:36) The Lord’s Prayer: ‘Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.’ After the prayer, Jesus adds: ‘If you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father also will forgive you; but if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.’ (Matthew 6:14-15) The parable of the unforgiving servant (Matthew 18:21-35): the servant forgiven an enormous debt refused to forgive a small one. Peter asked how many times to forgive; Jesus said seventy times seven. We can’t keep God’s mercy while refusing it to others.”", "Say: “What forgiveness is not. It’s not saying the wrong didn’t matter. It’s not forgetting. It’s not necessarily reconciliation: reconciliation needs the other party’s repentance and trust rebuilt, and in cases of abuse, safety comes first. It’s not a feeling. It’s not condoning. It doesn’t cancel justice or consequences. What it is: a decision to release the person from a debt of vengeance and resentment, to wish them well, and to entrust justice to God. It’s a process, often repeated. The Catechism (CCC 2843-2845): ‘It is not in our power not to feel or to forget an offense; but the heart that offers itself to the Holy Spirit turns injury into compassion and purifies the memory in transforming the hurt into intercession.’ Practically: pray for the person, name the hurt honestly, ask for the grace, and take a step: a note, a prayer, a boundary.”", "Say: “Mercy more broadly: the works of mercy, corporal (feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, clothe the naked, shelter the homeless, visit the sick, visit the imprisoned, bury the dead) and spiritual (instruct the ignorant, counsel the doubtful, admonish the sinner, bear wrongs patiently, forgive offenses, comfort the afflicted, pray for the living and dead). Pope Francis’s Jubilee of Mercy (2015-16) renewed this emphasis: ‘Mercy is the very foundation of the Church’s life.’ Receiving mercy changes us. A person who has been forgiven is slow to condemn. Practical step: choose a work of mercy to practice this week. If someone hurt you deeply, you might need a counselor as well as prayer; forgiveness is not a substitute for healing. Corrie’s story shows the Holy Spirit gives what we can’t manufacture.”"],
    ask=["What does Jesus say about forgiving others? (We must, as God forgives us.)", "Is forgiveness a feeling? (No; it is an act of the will, helped by grace.)", "Does forgiveness always mean reconciliation? (No; reconciliation also requires repentance and trust, and safety comes first.)"],
    practice=["Write the name (privately) of a person you find hard to forgive. Under it, write: What happened? What do I want? What can I hand to God?", "In pairs, share (without names) one thing that makes forgiveness hard, and one thing that helps.", "Pray together for those who have hurt us: “Father, forgive them” (Luke 23:34).", "Choose one corporal or spiritual work of mercy to do this week."],
    close="Pray the Our Father slowly, pausing at the line about forgiveness. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to take the step this week.")),

'YA-5-1': dict(
  saint="The woman who touched Jesus’ cloak: she believed that a touch could heal, and it did.",
  tryit=["Describe one sacrament as sign, effect and cause.", "Say why Christ chose material things.", "Name the signs you notice in daily life."],
  saintStory="Mark and Luke both tell the story. A woman had suffered from bleeding for twelve years. She had spent all she had on doctors and only grew worse. Under the Law, her condition made her ritually unclean, cut off from worship and from touching others. In a crowd pressing around Jesus, she decided: “If I touch even his garments, I shall be made well.” She came up behind him and touched the fringe of his cloak. At once her bleeding stopped.\nJesus stopped and asked, “Who touched my garments?” His disciples said, “You see the crowd pressing around you, and yet you say, ‘Who touched me?’” But he knew that power had gone out from him. The woman, trembling, came and fell at his feet and told him the whole truth. He said, “Daughter, your faith has made you well; go in peace, and be healed of your disease.”\nHe called her “daughter,” restoring her to the community. The story shows the logic of the sacraments: a material thing, touched in faith, carries the saving power of Christ. The Church has seen in this a picture of what the sacraments do.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up an object with a personal meaning (a ring, a photo, a letter) and ask the group: “Why does a physical thing carry so much meaning? What is the difference between this and a plain piece of metal?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “A sacrament, the Catechism says, is ‘an efficacious sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is dispensed to us.’ (CCC 1131) A sign: a visible thing pointing to something invisible (water for cleansing and life; bread for nourishment; oil for strength). Efficacious: it really does what it signs. Instituted by Christ: only God can attach grace to a sign. Entrusted to the Church: she celebrates it. There are seven: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist; Penance, Anointing of the Sick; Holy Orders, Matrimony.”", "Say: “Why material signs? Because we are body and soul. God made matter good, and in the Incarnation, the Word became flesh. Jesus’ humanity is the primary sacrament: the visible face of the invisible God. He touched and healed (the leper, the blind man, the woman who touched him), used mud and saliva, washed feet, broke bread. After his Ascension, ‘what was visible in our Savior has passed over into his mysteries’ (Leo the Great). The Church is the sacrament of Christ in the world (LG 1). So the sacraments are the continuation of Christ’s touch. Each has matter (water, bread and wine, oil, laying on of hands) and form (the words). The minister must intend what the Church does.”", "Say: “How do they work? Ex opere operato (‘by the work done’): the grace is given by Christ himself acting through the rite, not dependent on the minister’s holiness. But their fruit depends on our disposition: faith, repentance and openness. The woman with the flow of blood exercised faith. Sacraments aren’t magic. Three sacraments leave a permanent mark (the character): Baptism, Confirmation and Orders; they can’t be repeated. The sacraments give sanctifying grace (a share in God’s life) and sacramental grace specific to each. They are also signs of the Church’s unity, and we receive them within the community. Worship is not merely something we do for God but something he does for us. Practical: approach the sacraments with attention. Notice the signs (water, oil, bread, hands). Come with faith.”"],
    ask=["What is a sacrament? (An efficacious sign of grace instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church.)", "Why does God use material things? (We are body and soul; the Word became flesh.)", "What does ex opere operato mean? (The sacrament works by Christ’s action, not the minister’s holiness.)"],
    practice=["Read Mark 5:25-34. Notice every physical detail (touch, cloak, blood, trembling).", "Make a chart of the seven sacraments: the sign (matter), the words (form), the grace.", "In pairs, choose one sacrament and explain it as if to a curious friend.", "Write one sign you will pay closer attention to at the next Mass, such as water, oil or bread."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, we reach out to touch you. Heal us.” Sign of the Cross. Invite everyone to make the Sign of the Cross with holy water on entering church this week.")),

'YA-5-2': dict(
  saint="Margaret Mary Alacoque: a French Visitation nun who heard, “Behold the Heart that has so loved men.”",
  tryit=["Lay out the biblical case for the Real Presence.", "Name the strongest objection.", "Write a prayer of adoration."],
  saintStory="Margaret Mary Alacoque was born in 1647 in Burgundy, France. Her father died when she was eight, and she suffered illness and family troubles. At twenty-four she entered the Visitation convent at Paray-le-Monial. She was shy and sometimes misunderstood by the other nuns.\nBetween 1673 and 1675, she reported a series of visions of Christ. In the most famous, while praying before the Blessed Sacrament, she saw Jesus show his heart, burning with love, and say, “Behold this Heart which has so loved men that it spared nothing, even exhausting and consuming itself, in order to testify to them its love. And in return I receive from the greater part only ingratitude.” He asked for a feast in honor of the Sacred Heart and for devotion to receive Communion on the first Friday of each month, and to spend an hour in prayer on Thursday nights.\nHer superiors were skeptical, but her confessor, the Jesuit Claude la Colombière, supported her. After her death in 1690, the devotion spread. She was canonized in 1920. The Church has approved the devotion, though it doesn’t require belief in the private revelations.",
  script=dict(
    open="Place a piece of bread on a small plate in the center of the circle. Say, “What would you have to believe for this to be Christ’s body? What would you have to doubt?” Let a few people share.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that in the Eucharist, Jesus Christ is truly, really and substantially present: body, blood, soul and divinity, under the appearances of bread and wine (CCC 1374). This is the Real Presence. The Council of Trent used the word transubstantiation: the substance of bread and wine is changed into Christ’s body and blood, while the appearances remain. Scriptural basis: John 6:51-58. Jesus said ‘the bread which I shall give for the life of the world is my flesh,’ and when the Jews objected, he insisted: ‘Truly, truly, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you.’ He used a strong verb for ‘eat’ (trogein, chew). Many disciples left, and he didn’t call them back to explain it was a symbol.”", "Say: “More evidence. The Last Supper: ‘This is my body… This is my blood of the covenant.’ (Matthew 26:26-28) Paul: ‘The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not a participation in the blood of Christ? The bread which we break, is it not a participation in the body of Christ?’ (1 Corinthians 10:16) And ‘whoever eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in an unworthy manner will be guilty of profaning the body and blood of the Lord.’ (11:27) Early Fathers: Ignatius of Antioch (c. 107) calls the Eucharist ‘the flesh of our Savior Jesus Christ’; Justin Martyr (c. 155): ‘not as common bread… but the flesh and blood of that Jesus who was made flesh’; Cyril of Jerusalem and Ambrose speak of the change. For fifteen centuries, nearly all Christians held a real presence. Disagreements arose in the 16th century.”", "Say: “Objections. ‘It’s a symbol.’ Reply: Jesus’ words, and the unanimous early witness. ‘It still looks like bread.’ Reply: the Church says the appearances remain; faith accepts Christ’s word. Aquinas: ‘Sight, touch and taste are deceived in thee, but hearing suffices.’ ‘Cannibalism.’ Reply: we receive Christ’s glorified body in a sacramental manner, not the flesh of a dead body. What follows? Adoration: we kneel before the Blessed Sacrament. Eucharistic hours and processions; Benediction. Reverence in reception: state of grace, one-hour fast, a bow. The devotion to the Sacred Heart: Jesus’ love for us in the Eucharist. The Eucharist makes us one: ‘Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body.’ (1 Corinthians 10:17). It sends us to the poor. Margaret Mary’s message is that this is a love story.”"],
    ask=["What does the Church mean by the Real Presence? (Christ is truly present, body, blood, soul and divinity, in the Eucharist.)", "Name a Scripture text for it. (John 6:51-58; Matthew 26:26-28; 1 Corinthians 10:16 and 11:27.)", "What is transubstantiation? (The substance changes; the appearances remain.)"],
    practice=["Read John 6:51-69 aloud, in turns. Mark the places where Jesus insists and where people leave.", "Write the Real Presence argument in five lines for a Protestant friend.", "Spend five minutes in silent adoration (before the Blessed Sacrament if possible), then write a prayer of adoration.", "Plan a holy hour or a first Friday visit this month."],
    close="Pray the Tantum Ergo or: “Jesus, I adore you, truly present.” Sign of the Cross. Offer a time to visit adoration together.")),

'YA-5-3': dict(
  saint="Vladimir of Kyiv: a pagan prince who sent envoys to compare religions and was baptized in 988.",
  tryit=["Trace your own initiation.", "Write what Baptism and Confirmation changed.", "Choose one way to live it out this month."],
  saintStory="Vladimir was prince of Kyiv, the leader of the Rus’, in the late tenth century. He came to power by war and by killing his brother. He was a pagan with a harem and built temples to the Slavic gods, even offering human sacrifice. But as ruler, he saw that his people needed a unifying faith. According to the Primary Chronicle, he sent envoys to investigate the religions of neighboring peoples: the Muslim Bulgars, the Jewish Khazars, the Latin Christians of Germany, and the Greeks of Constantinople.\nThe envoys were unimpressed by the first three. When they attended the liturgy at the Hagia Sophia in Constantinople, they reported: “We knew not whether we were in heaven or on earth. For on earth there is no such splendor or such beauty, and we are at a loss how to describe it. We only know that God dwells there among men.” In 988, Vladimir was baptized, took a Byzantine princess, Anna, as his wife, and ordered his people to be baptized in the Dnieper River.\nAfter his baptism, he changed. He gave up his other wives, fed the poor, built churches and schools, and became known as a merciful ruler. He died in 1015. He is honored as Equal-to-the-Apostles and as the founder of Christianity among the Slavs.",
  script=dict(
    open="Lay out any baptismal candle, certificate or photo that anyone brought. Invite each person to hold one item for a moment and say one thing it makes them remember about their own initiation.",
    teach=["Say: “The sacraments of Christian initiation are Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist. Together they ‘lay the foundations of every Christian life.’ (CCC 1212) Baptism: the door. It forgives original sin and all personal sin, gives new birth as God’s child, makes us a member of Christ and the Church, and marks us with an indelible character. Romans 6:3-4: we are buried with Christ and rise to new life. Confirmation: the sealing with the Holy Spirit, strengthening for witness. It completes Baptism. The Eucharist: food for the journey. In the early Church, adults received all three at the Easter Vigil. The Church today does the same for adults through RCIA.”", "Say: “What Baptism does in practice: You belong to God. ‘You are not your own; you were bought with a price.’ (1 Corinthians 6:19-20) You share Christ’s mission as priest, prophet and king. You are called to holiness. You have a family, the Church. Confirmation: you’ve received the Holy Spirit with his seven gifts (wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord) and you are sent to witness. Many people see Confirmation as a graduation from the Church. The Church sees it as a commissioning. The question: Are you living as one baptized, confirmed and fed? What would that look like? A faith that is public, personal, and lived. Are you still in contact with your godparents or sponsor?”", "Say: “Practical living. Remember your Baptism: Do you know the date? Celebrate it as a spiritual birthday. Renew your baptismal promises regularly, especially at Easter. Use holy water. Know your patron saint. Use your gifts: ask the Holy Spirit daily. Participate in parish life: join a group, serve, invite. Go to Mass weekly, Confession regularly. If you were baptized but never confirmed, talk to your parish about adult Confirmation. If you were not baptized, RCIA is available. If you know someone who left, be a bridge. Vladimir’s story shows that baptism can mark a real turning point. Don’t treat initiation as an event in the past but as a continuing process.”"],
    ask=["What are the sacraments of initiation? (Baptism, Confirmation and Eucharist.)", "What does Confirmation do? (Completes Baptism; seals with the Holy Spirit for witness.)", "How can you live your Baptism now? (Remember it, renew promises, use your gifts, take part in the Church.)"],
    practice=["Find your baptism and confirmation dates (or ask a family member). Write them down.", "Read the baptismal promises aloud as a group, and answer ‘I do.’", "Write down how you see your gifts from Confirmation at work, and one place you could use them.", "Choose one concrete way to live your initiation: join a group, serve, or invite someone."],
    close="Renew baptismal promises together and bless yourselves with holy water. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to celebrate their baptismal anniversary.")),
}
